Compile/run:
     - Compile using
            make CalcYouLater
     - run by 
            ./CalcYouLater


Program Purpose: Create a calculator that processes inputed Datum and adds
*                them to a a stack and processes commands preformed on the 
*                stored Datum. Datum stored can be either of type bool, int, or
*                a an rString storing a series of commands in postfix or a file
*                to read commands from. 


Acknowledgements: Caroline for helping me during the Design Check Off. Calvin
for helping me with a cerr.eof error. 

Files: 

main.cpp:
     Main file simple driver function that run the MetroSim simulator.

RPNCalc.cpp:
    Implementation of RPNCalc class. Main functionality was to process all 
    commands and run the appropraite helper functions based on input.

RPNCalc.h:
    interface of the RPNCalc class
*           An instance of this class creates a calculator where the user is 
*           able to provide input in the form of Datum (which can be a bool,
*           int, or rString) and this data will be stored, with the most 
*           recent at the top and the oldest input at the bottom. Also,
*           the user is able to input commands that wille be completement on 
*           the top and potentially second or third elements. These commands 
*           included arithmetic and boolean calculations, removal or addition
*           of new elements, manipulation of the placement of elements, and 
*           execution of a RPN string being stored. It is implemented with the
*           use of an instance of DatumStack to achieve the First in and First
*           out effect, where the top most element is the most recent element
*           inputted. 

parser.h:   
*           interface of the parser class
*           reads in an RPN String inputted bt the user  

parser.cpp: 
*           implementation of parser.h
*           reads in an RPN String inputted by the user  

DatumStack.h:
*           Interface of the DatumStack data structure 
*           The DatumStack class represents a stack storing Datum. It is able
*           to push new elements to the top, pop top elements off, pop all 
*           elements off (clear), give the user the size, top elements, and 
*           a bool whether it is empty. It is represented using a list of 
*           type Datum.

DatumStack.cpp:
    *  Implementation of the DatumStack Class 

exec.cyl, if.cyl, fib.cyl, fib_debug, fact.cyl 
        tester files that test a certain aspect of the code



Note:  the program should handle all cases.

F. 
Data Structures:

I used various different data structures in this project.

I used a list in order to create my DatumStack class. The 
DatumStack classes uses the built in c++ std::list in order to create a 
stack where Datum are stored and only the top Datum (last inputed Datum) can be 
accessed, the top Datum maybe removed, and a new Datum may be added to
the top. I used a list here because the complexity of completuing all necessary
tasks is the same for both ArrayLists and LinkedLists, so it did not matter,
which is used, and since I had more experience using LinkedLists from the first
project I decided to use it again. Another situation where to use a stack would
be when writing an email inbox. Since newer emails are placed at the top of the
inbox they can be perfectly represented by a stack. A third situation would be
when trying to keep track of edits made so that undo and redo buttons work, the
most recent edits would go at the top and the undo and redo buttons would want
to access the most recent edits to fix.

Algorithm Overview:

An interesting algorithm was the code that I wrote for the exec function. I 
feel like it really showed why using a stack was the optimal way to complete
this project. I also thing pushing each element onto the stack and then 
processing commands that way is such a smarter way to code this project than 
simply traversing the RString, which was my first thought to do when I was 
reading the spec of the project. The stack method was simpler and more elegant
way to do it. The second algoirthm I found interesting was the file command 
because I liked how I was able to use the same code that I had prior and just
simply read in from the file and pass it into the function I was using before.
This offered a much more modular and efficient solution than I initially 
thought I would need to write when I was planning my code. 

G. Testing:

DatumStack and Parser

For phase 1 I used unit tests to test my DatumStack and Parser classes. 
I first confirmed that I would be able to create instances of both the classes. 
Then I confirmed that the non default constrtor function in the DatumStack
functions works by attempting to create an instance and passinig in an array
of Datum. Then, I confirmed that the top function works.I did this by creating 
an instance with an array of Datum and then called top() and made sure that the
Datum had the correct data stored. Then I tested isEmpty(), by first creating 
an empty instance and testing that isEmpty would return true and in another
test function I created an instance with an array of Datum and made sure 
isEmpty() return false. I then tested size by creating an instance with an 
array again and confirming that size() was returning the correct size. Then 
tested clear by intializing an instance with an array and then calling clear() 
and calling isEmpty to check that the stack was now empty. Then tested top by 
firstly creating an instance with an array and then calling top to confirm the 
last element of the array was being returned, the second way I tested was to 
try accessing the top of an empty stack and catching the error and confirming 
that it was being thrown with the correct error message. Then I did the same 
process for pop() where I tested it with first a regular case to see if it 
removed the top element, and then having it pop an empty stack to see if the 
proper error and message was being thrown. Finally tested push() to see if the 
new element was being added to the top of the stack. I then wrote a couple 
unit tests for parseRString() and would feed it istringstreams where the 
rpnStrings were nested and across multiple lines, to confirm the parserString 
would be able to format it all on one line. 

RPNCalc

I tested RPNCalc by creating various .cyl files that would test various
functions and then running it on my program and the demo program, then 
redirecting output and error output, and finally diffing to make sure my code
produced the same ouput and error messages as the demo. For example, I have a 
file called if.cyl. Within that file I have multiple tests on the if function. 
These include a basic if use, a more complex if use, and a couple tests where 
the stack does not have the proper Datum types on the top to use if. Each
test also has print statements to make sure that my code is popping off all the
correct Datum throughout the program. I would then run ./cylc to convert if.cyl
to if.cylc and then create an if command in my make file that would run the 
test on my code and the demo, redirecting output to one file and error output
to another and then diff checking both. I repeated this process for all large 
functions that I had in my RPNCalc class. 

